WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.896 --> 00:00:07.330
CHRISTOPHER SANSON: All right.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.140
Hi, everyone.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.260
Welcome to our talk today.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.330
We're going to talk about
continuous integration

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.430
and continuous
delivery solutions

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.370
for hybrid and
multi-cloud customers.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.520
We're very excited to have you
here again on day two of Next,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.970
and for sticking with us.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.040
Yesterday, if you
happened to catch it,

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.670
we did a talk on
CI/CD for serverless.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.180
That was focused on sort
of cloud-native, modern,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.430
serverless applications
and infrastructure.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.380
This one is sort of
kind of the opposite.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.020
This one is focused on
large enterprises, companies

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.510
who are living in
sort of a world

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.810
where you have on-prem
applications as well as cloud,

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.270
and you have different services
running on multiple clouds,

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:47.686
and kind of some
of the challenges

00:00:47.686 --> 00:00:51.040
that may present
around developer

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.450
tooling in those ecosystems.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:58.180
So this is a stat that Urs
actually shared yesterday

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.160
from RightScale.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.209
80% of enterprises are adopting
a hybrid multi-cloud strategy.

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:05.680
And I think there's
a lot of talk

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.470
over time around different
words around cloud-native

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.810
and Tier 1 and Tier
2 and lift-and-shift

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.400
and all these different terms.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.010
And I think the world
that we've arrived at now

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.020
is that this is just
kind of the new normal

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.500
for a lot of different reasons,
whether it's cost or control

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.740
or not really seeing the
benefit to moving quite yet.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.160
And also sort of
leveraging the best

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.320
aspects of the different
cloud providers.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.690
Increasingly, enterprises
are adopting strategies

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.970
where they're deploying
different services

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:35.920
to different places,
and there's a lot

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.200
of challenges
between interacting

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.040
between on-prem
resources and the cloud.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.385
So what do we mean by
hybrid and multi-cloud?

00:01:45.385 --> 00:01:47.510
It's one of those words,
kind of like "serverless,"

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.254
kind of like "DevOps," that
depending on who you ask

00:01:50.254 --> 00:01:51.920
and what time of day
it is, you probably

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.710
get a different definition.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.460
I think the first thing
that comes to mind

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.099
is running applications.

00:01:57.099 --> 00:01:58.640
So we've had a bunch
of announcements

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.317
so far around GKE On-Prem,
serverless, things like that.

00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:05.150
And that's often where
your mind goes first.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.509
You think about applications,
so running Kubernetes on-prem,

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.560
running Kubernetes
on Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.290
or running it on other clouds.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.080
And for that, if you've
sort of looked at Google

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.790
or been in the space
long, you might

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:18.290
be familiar with Spinnaker.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.680
Spinnaker is our open-source,
multi-cloud, continuous

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.390
delivery platform that we
co-develop with Netflix.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.390
So Netflix started this.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.030
It's what they use for all
their continuous delivery.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:30.489
And there are some
other talks that

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:32.863
are going to be really focused
really explicitly on this.

00:02:32.863 --> 00:02:34.520
Target is giving a
talk this afternoon,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.010
that should be really great,
about how they use Spinnaker

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:41.420
to deploy to both their on-prem
retail stores and the cloud.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.750
And it's really set up for
sort of complex sequencing

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.060
and rollout orchestration.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.060
So these are just kind
of all the different use

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.730
cases, though, that often fall
under the hybrid umbrella.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.440
As we talk to customers
and we say, well,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.157
what do you mean by hybrid?

00:02:57.157 --> 00:02:58.740
We get all sorts of
different answers.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.480
So things around multi-cloud
deployment, edge computing,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.262
invoking legacy dependencies.

00:03:04.262 --> 00:03:05.720
But for today,
we're going to focus

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.010
on largely the context of CI,
and continuous integration.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.176
So for CI workloads,
they look a lot different

00:03:12.176 --> 00:03:13.550
than running
applications, right?

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.072
They're sort of more batch-like.

00:03:15.072 --> 00:03:17.030
You have to deal with
things like configuration

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.990
management, peak workloads,
multiple platforms, things

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.050
like that.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:26.390
And what we find is that a lot
of companies who have on-prem

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.750
build dependencies-- so
things like GitLab or GitHub

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.330
Enterprise or Bitbucket
Enterprise or On-Prem

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.240
Artifactory-- at
build time, you need

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.760
to be able to securely
access these resources so

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.950
that you can actually
build and test your code.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.520
The problem is that, because
these things are on-prem,

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.050
you need to securely
access them,

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.700
you are now sort
of in the business

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.436
of having to run your
own CBOT CI server farm.

00:03:50.436 --> 00:03:52.310
If everything is in the
cloud, then of course

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.780
you can use a
cloud-hosted solution

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.988
and offload a lot of that
management and architecture

00:03:55.988 --> 00:03:57.044
to someone else.

00:03:57.044 --> 00:03:59.210
But the problem is that if
any one of these things--

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:02.810
source code, artifacts,
other things like databases

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.100
and license servers--

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:09.730
are on-prem, then your CI server
needs to be on-prem as well.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.362
And as we talk to customers,
really what they kind of want

00:04:12.362 --> 00:04:13.320
is something like this.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:14.670
They don't really want
to necessarily have

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.510
to move their source
code to the cloud.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.200
They don't want to have to
sort of move license servers

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:19.839
to the cloud.

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:22.005
Or maybe they just can't,
with the way their company

00:04:22.005 --> 00:04:22.770
is set up.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.540
But they would love
to be able to offload

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.040
all of that infrastructure,
and that they're

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.780
investing too many
resources in order

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.230
to scale up to the number of
builds that they're running.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.650
So we're going to talk a
little bit about that today.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.141
One of the announcements
earlier today--

00:04:37.141 --> 00:04:37.640
oh.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.350
And so one of the
takeaways, I think,

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.089
is that you can use managed
CI with on-prem resources.

00:04:43.089 --> 00:04:44.880
And we're going to give
a demo a little bit

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.490
later and talk about some new
solutions for Google Cloud

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.154
about how this is possible.

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:50.820
If you were at the
keynote this morning,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.486
you saw one of the announcements
that we're really excited about

00:04:53.486 --> 00:04:54.321
is around Knative.

00:04:54.321 --> 00:04:56.070
So if you are in the
category where you're

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.270
running workloads on-prem
and on top of Kubernetes,

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.760
this is a new series of
open-source components

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.680
that adds a new
layer of primitives

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.220
on top of Kubernetes, one of
which is called Knative Build.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.200
And that lets you--

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.410
is more of a
first-class way to find

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.600
builds that can run
on top of Kubernetes.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.060
But for our talk, we're
going to focus on, how can I

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.160
offload this all together?

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.726
So before we jump
into that, I want

00:05:23.726 --> 00:05:24.850
to do a quick little story.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.349
And this is going
to be interactive.

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:28.560
I'm going to describe
some of the conversations

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.110
we had with a customer about
their current situation.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.167
And if anyone can sort
of guess who this is,

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:35.250
I want you to please feel
free to raise your hand,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.800
shout it out, take a wild guess.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.300
There are no wrong answers.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.280
So this company had a
self-hosted CI system,

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:45.900
and they had a couple
developer teams

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.449
who were building software.

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:49.740
And they were running tests
on their local workstation,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.448
and they were building
software, and that

00:05:51.448 --> 00:05:52.710
was kind of working fine.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.940
But after a while, they
were having trouble.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.950
They were having inconsistent
build environments.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.240
They were getting
weird irregularities

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.364
between their workstations.

00:05:58.364 --> 00:06:01.710
And so they decided, hey, let's
set up a dedicated CI server.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.570
So they set up a CI server farm.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.670
They installed Jenkins,
or something like that,

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.086
and sort of wrapped
it with things

00:06:07.086 --> 00:06:08.227
like events and workflow.

00:06:08.227 --> 00:06:10.560
And out the other side, they
would have things like logs

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.850
and build artifacts.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.502
And this worked pretty
well for a while.

00:06:14.502 --> 00:06:16.710
And other companies and
teams inside the organization

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.709
said, hey, this is looking
actually pretty good.

00:06:18.709 --> 00:06:19.930
We want to get in on this.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.920
So they started saying, could
we use the same CI system?

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:23.940
And so they joined.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.854
And they started sending
even more builds.

00:06:25.854 --> 00:06:28.020
And what happened is that
the more builds they sent,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.478
they started running
into problems,

00:06:29.478 --> 00:06:31.560
things like bottlenecks,
things like queuing.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.101
And so they said,
hey, you know what?

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:35.382
We should get a dedicated
team that can handle this.

00:06:35.382 --> 00:06:36.840
So maybe it's
somebody on the team.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.173
Maybe it's a new dedicated team.

00:06:38.173 --> 00:06:40.370
But someone said, hey,
you're responsible now

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.120
for making sure that
this thing is working

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.226
and that our developers can
continue iterating on code.

00:06:45.226 --> 00:06:46.600
And this worked
pretty well, too.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.320
But the company kept growing,
and the team kept growing,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.329
and so they kept
sending more builds.

00:06:51.329 --> 00:06:52.620
And this introduced new issues.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.786
They were building all sorts
of new, different types

00:06:54.786 --> 00:06:56.140
of applications.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:57.932
So they kind of continued
to grow the team.

00:06:57.932 --> 00:06:59.890
And around this time,
they said, you know, this

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.590
is kind of actually
like a formal job.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.570
So we should sort of
give this team a name.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:05.970
So we called it the Platforms
team, or the Tools team,

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:06.689
or the Ops team.

00:07:06.689 --> 00:07:08.730
And they were really
responsible for building out

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.814
this infrastructure
for the developers.

00:07:10.814 --> 00:07:12.480
And they said, you
know, not only can we

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.100
actually manage our
machines, but we can actually

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.480
add a lot of value here.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.250
So we could do
things like provide

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:20.176
more logic around releases and
rollouts, things like Canaries.

00:07:20.176 --> 00:07:22.050
We can do things like
security and analytics,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.383
so that we can measure things
like mean time to release,

00:07:24.383 --> 00:07:25.595
improve over time.

00:07:25.595 --> 00:07:27.720
And management said, you
know, that sounds actually

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.320
like a pretty good idea.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.340
So we grew that
team a little bit.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:32.310
So that kept growing.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:33.690
But the company kept growing.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.100
We kept adding more developers.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.320
We kept adding more builds.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.234
And at this point in
time, they said, gee,

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:40.650
it looks like we've
actually built

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.660
an entire CI/CD infrastructure.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.086
We should give
this thing a name.

00:07:44.086 --> 00:07:45.960
So we call it the thing
like the Accelerator,

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.780
or the Pipeline Factory,
or something like that.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.930
And they sort of productize
that internally, present it

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.900
to developers, and
sort of say, you know,

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.650
you don't need really
to think about this.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:59.430
This is kind of our own
little internal CI product.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:01.630
So the team grew even more.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.780
But what happened is
this kind of never ended.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:04.710
The team kept growing.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.000
The builds kept coming.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.624
And the builds, they
just-- the problem

00:08:07.624 --> 00:08:09.360
is they never
really stop, right?

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.444
So what we found is that,
in talking to customers,

00:08:11.444 --> 00:08:13.568
the number of builds and
tests that they're running

00:08:13.568 --> 00:08:15.030
are actually growing
superlinearly

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.140
to the size of their team.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:17.640
So if their engineering
team is kind

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.940
of going like this, because of
modern best practices like CI,

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.232
their build volume is actually
kind of going like this.

00:08:23.232 --> 00:08:25.148
And what's happening is
that you're constantly

00:08:25.148 --> 00:08:26.067
racing against time.

00:08:26.067 --> 00:08:28.650
And so you could sort of see the
trend line, where it's going.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.410
And your infrastructure
is just keeping up

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.360
with the amount of
builds that you can do.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.860
And what we found is that
customers are actually

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.030
doing kind of crazy things.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:37.530
They're actually doing
things like throttling

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.120
the number of builds
that their developers run

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.267
by not adding new features.

00:08:40.267 --> 00:08:41.850
So they're sort of
discouraging builds

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:45.590
because the infrastructure
can't really handle it.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:46.100
All right.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.100
So who was it?

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.490
Anyone have any guesses?

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:50.180
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:51.138
Let me ask it this way.

00:08:51.138 --> 00:08:53.270
Does this sound like
any of you here?

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:54.270
Yeah, it's everybody.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:55.280
This is everybody.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.214
Everybody has gone through this.

00:08:57.214 --> 00:08:59.630
There's a Googler, he calls
this the Rube Goldberg Machine

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:00.590
builder problem.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.720
So if you're familiar with
Rube Goldberg machines,

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.178
we basically keep
building machines

00:09:04.178 --> 00:09:05.475
that build other machines.

00:09:05.475 --> 00:09:07.100
And it starts innocently
enough, right?

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.770
You're like, oh, I need
to run a couple builds.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:10.490
Oh, I need to run
a couple triggers.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.470
Oh, now I want to
get some metadata,

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:13.460
and I want to do
some more workflow.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:15.584
And before you know it,
you've reinvented the wheel

00:09:15.584 --> 00:09:19.030
and you've created a whole
CI/CD infrastructure.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.800
And yes, Google was
absolutely a right answer.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:22.700
We're no exception.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:27.260
Back in 2015, we were
running 800,000 builds a day.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:30.140
In 2018, that number is
already up to 4 million.

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.870
Our engineering team has
not grown at this same pace.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:36.032
And honestly, this number
continues to go up today.

00:09:36.032 --> 00:09:39.020
And that's because
the builds never stop.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.630
We don't see this trend
ending any time soon.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:44.425
We have things like now
ML workloads, and IoT,

00:09:44.425 --> 00:09:45.050
and serverless.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.880
And the number of
builds and tests

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.757
are just going to continue
to grow faster and faster.

00:09:50.757 --> 00:09:53.090
So this is kind of the first
takeaway of our talk today,

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:55.550
is that CI, I think
fundamentally,

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:56.982
is a compute problem.

00:09:56.982 --> 00:09:59.190
We tend to think of it as
kind of a workflow problem.

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:01.315
It's like, oh, if I can
write just the right number

00:10:01.315 --> 00:10:03.530
of scripts, and automate
them in just the right way,

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.570
I can run CI/CD.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:06.990
And that's absolutely true.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.740
The problem is that
you then actually need

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.490
to run those
workloads somewhere.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.630
And CI compute looks
really different.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:13.610
It's batch workloads.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:14.870
It's configuration management.

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:19.210
It's crazy things like
nested virtualization.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.140
And this has a real cost to it.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:23.320
So McKinsey did a
recent study and found

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.230
that application developers
spend a third of their time

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:27.790
as system engineers.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:31.455
So this is things like debugging
builds, standing up servers.

00:10:31.455 --> 00:10:33.580
I don't know, has anyone
here ever kind of upgraded

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.038
the version of Jenkins that
was running and just completely

00:10:36.038 --> 00:10:37.431
wiped out their entire system?

00:10:37.431 --> 00:10:37.930
Yeah.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:38.430
Yeah.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.640
Yeah, I have.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.005
So how can we sort
of move past this?

00:10:43.005 --> 00:10:44.380
So if we look back
at our diagram

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.960
here, a lot of customers
that we talk to say,

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.720
there's a lot of value that
we're bringing to our company

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.880
around kind of the
releases and rollouts

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:53.200
and the product itself.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.840
But this whole middle area
of machine management, we'd

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.340
really love to
move to the cloud.

00:10:57.340 --> 00:10:59.080
We'd like to offload
all of this and just

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.044
stop having to think about it.

00:11:01.044 --> 00:11:02.710
And then that lets
us get back to things

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:04.400
that really are more important.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.000
So things like improving
our mean time to release,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.190
reducing vulnerabilities,
keeping our company out

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:12.880
of the headlines with
downtime and things like that.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.480
So the good news is
that we're here to help.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.407
And we're introducing
this week Cloud Build.

00:11:18.407 --> 00:11:19.740
So this was announced yesterday.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:24.120
It's a hosted CI/CD platform for
increasing developer velocity,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:27.480
so that you and your teams can
develop more quickly and more

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.040
safely.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:32.100
So as a brief history, we
launched this a year ago

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:33.450
as Container Builder.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:37.410
Container Builder was a hosted
solution to build Docker images

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.500
and push them up to Container
Registry in a safe and reusable

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:41.017
way.

00:11:41.017 --> 00:11:43.350
And our vision for this was
always a little bit grander.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.420
We realized that not only
can we build containers

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.640
using this approach,
but we can do things

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.890
like build tarballs
and binaries and run

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:51.890
sort of different tasks.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:54.060
And you could point it out
at Git Repository, set up

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.810
a trigger so that when
you did a git push,

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.695
it would automate that for you.

00:11:58.695 --> 00:12:00.070
With the Cloud
Build announcement

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.780
today, we have a couple features
as well that we're announcing.

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.780
The first is that you'll
notice that this is now

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:06.572
top level in the Cloud Console.

00:12:06.572 --> 00:12:08.530
So if you go to the Cloud
Console, under Tools,

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.810
you'll now see Cloud Build.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.120
We added support for
first-class artifacts,

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:16.120
so the same way you can build
and push images and associate

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.180
that with your builds, you
can now build other artifacts,

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.010
like binaries and
tarballs as well,

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.810
and push those up
to Cloud storage.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.110
We also added support
for file path triggers,

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:28.900
and this lets you
filter your triggers

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.880
based on certain files
or subdirectories that

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:32.034
have changed.

00:12:32.034 --> 00:12:34.450
And we have two other features
in private alpha right now.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.280
They're going to be coming soon.

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.680
One is a much deeper
integration with GitHub.

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.200
So we're adding native
support for pull requests,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.150
posting status back
to the Checks API

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:44.620
that they just
recently announced,

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.282
as well as creating triggers
programmatically via the API.

00:12:48.282 --> 00:12:49.740
And the last is a
brand-new results

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.660
UI that we demoed
yesterday, that

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.240
helps developers debug
builds faster and get

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:58.150
more of a dashboard-like
view over their projects.

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:00.840
So this all sounds
great, Christopher,

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.760
but I kind of
thought the talk was

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.890
about hybrid and multi-cloud.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:06.480
This doesn't really help me.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:08.130
This is still a
cloud-hosted service.

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:09.796
I still have fundamental
security issues

00:13:09.796 --> 00:13:12.720
about it interacting
with on-prem resources.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:15.390
So today, we're also excited
to announce something else--

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.320
Cloud Build Custom Workers.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:19.980
So Cloud Build Custom Workers
is a new feature of Cloud Build.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:21.450
It's in early access right now.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.490
It's going to be available soon.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.440
And what it does is
it lets you securely

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.185
connect Cloud Build workers
to your on-prem resources.

00:13:29.185 --> 00:13:31.560
So let's take a little bit of
a look at how this actually

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.950
works.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:34.662
So this is my GCP project here.

00:13:34.662 --> 00:13:36.120
And the way Cloud
Build works today

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:40.352
is that from my GCP project, I
request a build to Cloud Build.

00:13:40.352 --> 00:13:42.060
Cloud Build then
actually runs that build

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:45.180
on a worker VM, which is
just a Google Compute Engine

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.730
VM in a shared pool
that we manage,

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.920
and run every build on a new VM.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:51.350
So you send us a build request.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:55.290
We grab one of the VMs from the
worker pool, run your build,

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.559
and then spin it down.

00:13:56.559 --> 00:13:58.350
And so every build runs
on its own machine,

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:00.850
so it's completely
isolated and secure.

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:03.240
The problem is that,
in an on-prem scenario,

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.150
you're behind some firewall or
private network restrictions.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.710
So when that worker goes to
access your source code or push

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:10.845
to a registry, it can't.

00:14:10.845 --> 00:14:13.320
It hits the firewall,
doesn't have permissions.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:15.340
So that's not going to work.

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:18.330
So we needed to
do something else.

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:20.310
So with Custom Workers,
instead of running

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.100
builds on a shared worker
pool up in the cloud,

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.770
we're going to run workers
inside of a GCP project

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:29.070
that you own and control,
and run builds there.

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:31.900
Now, because this is
running in your GCP project,

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:34.320
you can use VPC, or
virtual private cloud,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.600
to connect that project
to your private network

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.190
through peering or sharing.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.860
And now that worker can
securely talk to and access

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.080
your on-prem resources.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.570
And what's really great
to realize about this

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.890
is that this is still
a managed service.

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:49.890
So these workers,
these GCE VMs that

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.620
are running inside your project,
you have complete control over.

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:54.810
You can access, you can
delete, you can configure.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:56.940
But they're completely
managed by Cloud Build.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.880
So you just tell us how big your
pool size you want it to be,

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.410
what kind of machine
type you use,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:03.870
and Cloud Build will still
be responsible for delegating

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:07.066
builds to it, spinning up new
VMS, maintaining that pool,

00:15:07.066 --> 00:15:08.940
managing the pool size,
making sure that it's

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.590
up, ready, and available.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.525
So let's actually
see this in action.

00:15:13.525 --> 00:15:15.650
So we're going to jump over
to a demo for a second.

00:15:21.864 --> 00:15:25.210
Thank you.

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:29.960
And so for this instance, we've
set up two projects in GCP.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:33.220
So the first project
is my on-prem project.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:38.170
And inside of this
project, I have

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.499
installed a GitLab instance.

00:15:40.499 --> 00:15:42.040
So I'm running a
self-hosted instance

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:45.070
of GitLab, where I have my
source code in my repository.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:48.400
I then have a separate GCP
project for my Custom Worker

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:50.000
pool.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:51.920
And inside of this
Custom Worker pool,

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:58.280
I'm going to use VPC peering
to set up a secure connection.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.530
So this is my network
that I've created.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.260
It's called My Network.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:05.840
And I connected it to
my GCP on-prem project.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:08.260
And if I go over to my
GCP on-prem project,

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:11.200
and I look at the
VPC network there,

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:15.440
we'll see that I've
created its equivalent,

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:17.500
so that now these two
networks are securely

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.467
talking to each other.

00:16:19.467 --> 00:16:21.300
And if I go back to my
Custom Worker project

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:25.770
here, and look under
Compute Engine,

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:27.840
we'll see our worker pool.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.840
So these are GCE
worker VMs that have

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.360
been spun up and managed
by Cloud Build inside

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:35.140
of your project.

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:37.026
So we should be good to go here.

00:16:37.026 --> 00:16:38.400
So if I go over
to our repository

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:41.400
here, so this is
our GitLab repo,

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.560
we have a pretty simple
application set up.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.096
We have a simple Ruby app.

00:16:45.096 --> 00:16:47.720
And let's just go in and make an
arbitrary change to our Docker

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:48.220
file.

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:56.530
So I'm going to
commit that change.

00:16:56.530 --> 00:17:00.450
And then if I go back to my
project and look at Cloud Build

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:03.670
here, we should see
that a new build

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.119
has successfully kicked off.

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:09.060
If I jump in here,
this is our build,

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.828
and we're going to
run a few steps here.

00:17:10.828 --> 00:17:11.869
So these are build steps.

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:13.285
This is the Cloud
Build convention

00:17:13.285 --> 00:17:15.953
for running sort of jobs or
tasks as part of your build.

00:17:15.953 --> 00:17:17.869
And if we step through
these, we're going to--

00:17:17.869 --> 00:17:18.849
oop, it's already done.

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:20.040
It's pretty fast.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:21.810
Runs on Google network.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:24.014
We're going to fetch
credentials from cloud storage.

00:17:24.014 --> 00:17:25.680
We're going to decrypt
those credentials

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.200
with the built-in integration
with KMS, which is Google's

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:29.670
key management service.

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:32.190
We're then going to
configure Git, do a git

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:34.860
clone to fetch our source
code from the repo,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:39.520
and then build our image
and push it to the registry.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.716
And that all works pretty well.

00:17:41.716 --> 00:17:43.090
And the way that
we wired this up

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:47.320
is we use Cloud Functions to act
as a bridge between the source

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:50.032
repo event and the actual
invocation of the build.

00:17:50.032 --> 00:17:51.490
So if we go over
to Cloud Functions

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:54.220
as well in our
project, we previously

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:57.470
had set up this function.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:17:59.980
So this function listens for
webhook events from GitLab

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.150
and then runs a build.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:03.441
And so these are the same
build steps that we just saw.

00:18:03.441 --> 00:18:05.710
We fetch credentials,
we build the image,

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.950
and we push it to the registry.

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:13.780
And if we go back over to GitLab
and look at our integrations,

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:18.950
we'll see that we had previously
set up a webhook pointing

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:20.810
at that Cloud Function.

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:23.480
So now every time we make a
change and we do a push or tag

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:26.374
or do a merge event,
it'll send that webhook up

00:18:26.374 --> 00:18:27.290
to our Cloud Function.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:29.990
The Cloud Function will
hear it, kick off the build,

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:33.080
and run it on Cloud Build.

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:33.830
So that was great.

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:35.704
But we haven't actually
shown this running

00:18:35.704 --> 00:18:36.620
on our custom workers.

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.911
This would roughly look the
same if you were running it

00:18:38.911 --> 00:18:40.220
on the shared pool.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:43.760
So let's actually split this
out and do these side by side.

00:18:46.227 --> 00:18:47.560
So this is our worker pool here.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:52.890
And if we jump back
over to our project

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:57.570
here and go to our functions,
let's kick off a few builds

00:18:57.570 --> 00:19:00.370
and see this happening.

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:02.290
So I'm going to
run a couple tests.

00:19:02.290 --> 00:19:06.650
One, two, let's say four builds.

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:08.880
And go back to our
workers, and refresh here.

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:12.780
We should see, and
sure enough, our builds

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:15.430
are running on our
custom worker pool.

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:17.430
And if we keep refreshing,
we should ultimately

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:20.229
get to around four new VMs.

00:19:20.229 --> 00:19:22.020
And so what's happening
is that Cloud Build

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:24.600
has sent the build to
one of our GCE workers.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.300
And now it's in the background,
spinning up a new VM

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:30.210
to replace it so that the
next build that comes in,

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:33.019
the VM is pre-warmed
and ready to go.

00:19:33.019 --> 00:19:34.560
And if we keep
refreshing this, we'll

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:36.900
see this sort of
happen over time.

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:39.930
And we're up to now, yeah,
our four VMs running.

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:41.430
And if we kept going
with this, we'd

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:43.970
eventually see that it
would go back down to five.

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:45.690
As those builds would
complete, the VMs

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.010
would just be picked off.

00:19:49.225 --> 00:19:50.600
And if we go over
to our terminal

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:55.910
here, and do an API response
here, and hit our Git endpoint

00:19:55.910 --> 00:20:01.960
for the worker pool, we
can see the configuration

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.210
that we had previously
used to set up our pool.

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:05.860
So again, this is
Custom Workers.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:08.420
You are in full control
and you own this.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:11.470
So this is the project that
we had invoked this in.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:13.144
We set our worker count to five.

00:20:13.144 --> 00:20:14.560
We're running in
multiple regions,

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:18.790
for redundancy reasons, running
on n1-standard-1 machines.

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:20.976
And we set up our
own private network.

00:20:20.976 --> 00:20:23.600
Now, I really want to call out
a couple of the highlights here,

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:25.960
which I think are
particularly cool, even

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:27.880
outside of the on-prem stuff.

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:29.430
So this is all up to you.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.300
So we set our
worker pool at five,

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:33.580
so that-- you know, for
the sake of the demo.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:37.580
But this is really subject
to your own GCP quota.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:39.040
You can set this to five.

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:40.450
You can set it to 500.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:41.950
You can set it to 5,000.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:43.030
It doesn't really matter.

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:45.200
It's going to work.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.040
And the other part
is machine type.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:49.929
So if you're familiar
with Cloud Build,

00:20:49.929 --> 00:20:51.470
the shared pool that
we run builds on

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:54.470
are all n1-standard-1 machines,
and they're pre-warmed.

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:59.270
We do offer two higher CPU
machines, a high CPU eight

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:00.980
and a high CPU 32.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.180
But they're not pre-warmed.

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:03.770
So you have to wait
for spin-up time,

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:06.260
and usually the beefier machine
kind of makes up for it.

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:07.640
But we hear
frequently from users

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:09.223
that they have
different requirements.

00:21:09.223 --> 00:21:11.090
They want a machine
with more RAM.

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:13.340
They want a pre-warmed compute.

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:15.710
They may want, I
don't know, 64 cores.

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:17.030
Who knows?

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:19.160
So you can now completely
configure that yourself.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:22.170
So any GCE VM machine
type that's available,

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:23.930
you can configure
for your pool, so you

00:21:23.930 --> 00:21:25.700
have a pre-warmed
pool of any machine

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:28.340
type of any size you want,
that's dedicated just

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:29.790
for you and your builds.

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:38.198
So if we jump back over
to our project here,

00:21:38.198 --> 00:21:41.226
we're going to show kind of
one more interesting thing.

00:21:41.226 --> 00:21:43.600
So one of the things we hear
from customers a lot as well

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:47.590
is that, this is great, but
I'm not starting from scratch.

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:49.370
I already have a
CI system set up.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:50.260
I'm using Jenkins.

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:51.430
I'm using Buildkite.

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:52.960
I built my own homegrown system.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:54.160
Something like that.

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:57.197
And I really am sick and tired
of people trying to convince me

00:21:57.197 --> 00:21:58.780
that I need to rewrite
the whole thing

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.650
and migrate this whole workload,
and I need to do it by Q4.

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:02.900
It's just not going to happen.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:04.730
I still have a business to run.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:07.290
So what's great is that
because Cloud Build is--

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:09.260
it's also kind of a
build-as-a-service-type

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:09.760
solution.

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:10.810
It has an API.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:12.400
It works from the Cloud SDK.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:16.060
And you can use it as well
from your existing CI setups

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:18.572
to simply offload the
build infrastructure piece.

00:22:18.572 --> 00:22:20.530
So we're going to switch
to a different project

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:27.550
here, where we're using GitLab's
built-in CI/CD functionality.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:28.610
So GitLab's really great.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:30.430
It comes with a lot of
features, beyond just the source

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:31.888
repo, around
continuous integration

00:22:31.888 --> 00:22:32.888
and continuous delivery.

00:22:32.888 --> 00:22:34.596
And actually, I want
to give a shout out.

00:22:34.596 --> 00:22:36.460
We worked closely
with GitLab on this.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:39.790
I want to say thanks to
Joshua, Priyanka, and Sid,

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:42.700
who were really helpful in
helping set this all up.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:47.890
But if we go in here and we
look at our GitLab CI file,

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:49.680
we can see that we
are building a Docker

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:51.997
image using what I
think is probably

00:22:51.997 --> 00:22:53.580
the way the majority
of users do this,

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:56.300
which is running a CI runner--

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:59.820
probably a Kubernetes
instance running locally--

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.920
and doing a Docker
in Docker Build.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:04.830
So for anyone doing
Docker builds this way,

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:07.410
you're probably aware of some
of the security limitations

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:07.980
around this.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:09.840
So when you're doing
a Docker build,

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.048
you need access to the Docker
daemon, which gives you

00:23:12.048 --> 00:23:15.030
root access, which is sort
of a fundamental security

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.910
risk, in that malicious code
can potentially take over

00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:21.450
your node, break out of the box,
and introduce a lot of issues.

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:22.200
Now, we get it.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.540
This is sort of the way
that most people do it

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:28.620
because the idea of creating
your own pool of isolated VMs,

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:31.729
and spinning up and spinning
down VMs, is a lot of work.

00:23:31.729 --> 00:23:33.270
But if you're doing
Docker in Docker,

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:36.640
you're aware of some of the
latency and security issues.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:38.670
So what we can do, with
a few lines of code,

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:41.370
is simply swap this out and
offload all of our builds

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:42.470
to Cloud Build.

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:44.720
So we have a merge request
already queued up for this.

00:23:49.716 --> 00:23:51.090
So we're going to
jump over here.

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:56.150
And if we look at the
code changes that we made,

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:57.820
let's see how those
colors showed up.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:23:59.300
Pretty good.

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:01.050
We'll see that we
removed the steps where

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:03.850
we're doing Docker log-in,
Docker build, and Docker push.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:07.960
And we've replaced it with our
CloudBuild.YAML file, which is

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:11.230
the Cloud Build build config.

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:13.210
And we're going to
do the same steps.

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:15.910
We're going to do a Docker
log-in, Docker build,

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:17.820
and Docker push to the registry.

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:21.100
We're then going to submit the
build using the GCloud Build

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:21.750
Submit command.

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:28.600
And what this will do is
that this will move the job,

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.430
or the Docker build,
from our own CI runner

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:32.170
that we had to provision
and self-manage,

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:33.700
and over to Cloud Build.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:40.570
So if we go over to our
pipelines and run it,

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:45.890
and we're on our Cloud
Build branch here,

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:46.700
this will kick off.

00:24:49.697 --> 00:24:52.030
What's really great about
this is that, while this build

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:55.360
is running, you'll notice that
all the rest of my pipeline

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:56.690
hasn't had to change.

00:24:56.690 --> 00:24:59.106
So all the things that I'm
going to do after my image gets

00:24:59.106 --> 00:25:01.180
built-- things that
I'm doing in GitLab,

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.430
like checking code quality,
running container scans

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:04.960
and dependency scans--

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:06.340
just works as-is.

00:25:06.340 --> 00:25:07.870
So with very
minimal change, I've

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:11.710
been able to offload a
ton of the work and effort

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:15.045
that I normally have to do
in order to get this set up.

00:25:18.147 --> 00:25:19.480
So this is going to run through.

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:21.150
And if we wanted to
jump into as well,

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:23.156
we could see the logs here.

00:25:23.156 --> 00:25:24.780
You can see the steps
that are running.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.571
We're building the
image, pushing the image

00:25:26.571 --> 00:25:27.810
to the registry.

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:29.490
And it successfully succeeded.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:33.810
And this build, if we
refresh, should be done.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:38.370
And we've moved on with
the rest of our pipeline.

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:40.820
And we can see our worker
pool is back down to five now.

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:46.150
And what this lets us do is go
back over to our own project,

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:50.710
and go ahead and actually
delete our CI runner

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:53.150
that we had previously set
up because we don't need it

00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:53.765
anymore.

00:25:53.765 --> 00:25:54.640
It's not our problem.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:04.550
So if we switch back to
the presentation, let's

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.770
just sort of recap what we did.

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:10.910
We were able to spin down
our own Kubernetes cluster

00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:14.630
for doing builds, offload that
to the cloud, and using VPC,

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:18.470
connect multiple GCP projects
to a private network,

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:20.810
and use a hosted build
infrastructure as a service.

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:26.100
So what are some of the
benefits of Custom Workers?

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.110
First, it's managed
infrastructure.

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:30.930
Cloud Build is managing and
provisioning this pool for you,

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:33.760
so you don't have
to worry about it.

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.710
The second, I think it's
highly configurable.

00:26:35.710 --> 00:26:37.610
So we showed the API get call.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.390
You can pick your
machine type here.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:42.330
You can pick your pool size,
even configure your own network

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:43.230
settings.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.200
And this is just the beginning.

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:48.185
Having dedicated
pools per customer

00:26:48.185 --> 00:26:50.310
lets us do a lot of things
that, with shared pools,

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:51.445
aren't really feasible.

00:26:51.445 --> 00:26:53.070
And already from the
EAP, we're getting

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.960
a lot of feedback around
possibilities here,

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:58.412
so things like caching images,
adding persistent disks,

00:26:58.412 --> 00:27:00.870
letting you cache dependencies
on the worker ahead of time,

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:02.620
as opposed to fetching
them during builds,

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:03.690
to speed up builds.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:06.840
Also things like supporting
multiple regions, restricted IP

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:08.760
ranges, things like that.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.260
So a lot of this
is on our road map.

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:12.390
But it's definitely on
our radar, and something

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:13.830
we're hearing about
from customers

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:17.110
as an area of interest.

00:27:17.110 --> 00:27:19.570
And the last is, we should
talk about pricing, I guess,

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:20.860
I suppose.

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:22.444
So this is metered billing.

00:27:22.444 --> 00:27:24.610
And now, again, these are
Compute Engine VMs running

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:26.440
inside your GCP project.

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:28.900
So you pay for the
compute directly.

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:30.880
So whatever your
current agreement

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:35.000
is with Google for how you
pay for VMs, it's per minute,

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:38.860
and it's just subject to
the regular GCE usage.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:40.550
And there's no
additional overhead.

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:43.069
So you pay for the GCE
VMs and storage costs,

00:27:43.069 --> 00:27:44.860
and you basically get
Cloud Build for free.

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:49.920
So if we go back to
our diagram here,

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:51.870
and we sort of look
at what our goal was

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:54.360
that we were hoping
to achieve, we really

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:55.950
wanted to do
something like this.

00:27:55.950 --> 00:27:58.830
We wanted our build
dependencies to stay on-prem,

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:01.050
and we wanted
continuous integration

00:28:01.050 --> 00:28:04.310
to run in the cloud.

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:08.470
And I think we were able to
achieve that using Cloud Build.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.799
And what's really great
is that, as we showed,

00:28:10.799 --> 00:28:13.090
you don't actually have to
rewrite your whole entire CI

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:13.910
infrastructure.

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:16.300
So if you're currently running
on-prem CI with something

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:18.970
like Jenkins or TeamCity or you
wrote your own infrastructure

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:22.210
solution, you can
leverage Cloud Build

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:24.850
to replace your build
agents or your runners

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:28.230
in order to offload
that compute workload.

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:31.050
And because the worker is
running in an on-prem network,

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:36.490
you can now talk to resources
like repos and artifacts.

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:39.250
So one thing I do want to
touch upon before we go further

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:43.526
is how sort of Knative and some
of the other on-prem solutions

00:28:43.526 --> 00:28:44.650
kind of work in this world.

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:47.180
So we talked a little
bit at the beginning,

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:48.932
but this is for
people who really

00:28:48.932 --> 00:28:50.140
want to offload the workload.

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:52.690
If you really like the
idea of building out

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:54.940
your own infrastructure,
we definitely

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:56.740
recommend using
Kubernetes as sort

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:58.230
of an underlying component.

00:28:58.230 --> 00:28:59.980
It deals with a lot
of the issues for you,

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:02.870
things like auto-scaling,
things like caching.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:04.417
And if you take a
look at Knative,

00:29:04.417 --> 00:29:06.250
you'll find some new
primitives that make it

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:09.960
even easier for you to do that.

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:13.290
So if we look at some of
our takeaways, two big ones.

00:29:13.290 --> 00:29:17.002
CI is, I think, at the end of
the day, a compute problem.

00:29:17.002 --> 00:29:18.710
I think anyone here
who has to manage it,

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:21.830
is on the hook for it,
knows that you spent some

00:29:21.830 --> 00:29:24.380
nights and weekends
having to debug builds,

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:26.070
having to deal with
infrastructure.

00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:28.361
You can see the trend line
and know that sort of you're

00:29:28.361 --> 00:29:30.970
going to run out of
capacity by Q2 next year.

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:32.330
And that you need help.

00:29:32.330 --> 00:29:33.710
So that's what we're here for.

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:36.170
And Cloud Build Custom Workers
is a really great solution

00:29:36.170 --> 00:29:38.180
for enabling this.

00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:42.860
And this is because, again,
the builds never stop.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.830
So thank you very much.

00:29:44.830 --> 00:29:46.000
That was the presentation.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:47.500
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:50.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

